("Hang the boy, can't I never learn anything? Ain't he played me tricks) Tj
T*
(enough like that for me to be looking out for him by this time? But old) Tj
T*
(fools is the biggest fools there is. Can't learn an old dog new tricks,) Tj
T*
(as the saying is. But my goodness, he never plays them alike, two days,) Tj
T*
(and how is a body to know what's coming? He 'pears to know just how long) Tj
T*
(he can torment me before I get my dander up, and he knows if he can make) Tj
T*
(out to put me off for a minute or make me laugh, it's all down again and) Tj
T*
(I can't hit him a lick. I ain't doing my duty by that boy, and that's) Tj
T*
(the Lord's truth, goodness knows. Spare the rod and spile the child, as) Tj
T*
(the Good Book says. I'm a laying up sin and suffering for us both, I) Tj
T*
(know. He's full of the Old Scratch, but laws-a-me! he's my own dead) Tj
T*
(sister's boy, poor thing, and I ain't got the heart to lash him,) Tj
T*
(somehow. Every time I let him off, my conscience does hurt me so, and) Tj
T*
(every time I hit him my old heart most breaks. Well-a-well, man that is) Tj
T*
(born of woman is of few days and full of trouble, as the Scripture says,) Tj
T*
(and I reckon it's so. He'll play hookey this evening,[*] and I'll just) Tj
T*
(be obleeged to make him work, tomorrow, to punish him. It's mighty hard) Tj
T*
(to make him work Saturdays, when all the boys is having holiday, but he) Tj
T*
(hates work more than he hates anything else, and I've _got_ to do some) Tj
T*
(of my duty by him, or I'll be the ruination of the child.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
([*] Southwestern for "afternoon") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Tom did play hookey, and he had a very good time. He got back home) Tj
T*
(barely in season to help Jim, the small colored boy, saw next-day's wood) Tj
T*
(and split the kindlings before supper-at least he was there in time) Tj
T*
(to tell his adventures to Jim while Jim did three-fourths of the work.) Tj
T*
(Tom's younger brother \(or rather half-brother\) Sid was already through) Tj
T*
(with his part of the work \(picking up chips\), for he was a quiet boy,) Tj
T*
(and had no adventurous, trouble-some ways.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(While Tom was eating his supper, and stealing sugar as opportunity) Tj
T*
(offered, Aunt Polly asked him questions that were full of guile, and) Tj
T*
(very deep-for she wanted to trap him into damaging revealments. Like) Tj
T*
(many other simple-hearted souls, it was her pet vanity to believe she) Tj
T*
(was endowed with a talent for dark and mysterious diplomacy, and she) Tj
T*
(loved to contemplate her most transparent devices as marvels of low) Tj
T*
(cunning. Said she:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("Tom, it was middling warm in school, warn't it?") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("Yes'm.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("Powerful warm, warn't it?") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("Yes'm.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("Didn't you want to go in a-swimming, Tom?") Tj
T*
