(A bit of a scare shot through Tom-a touch of uncomfortable suspicion. He) Tj
T*
(searched Aunt Polly's face, but it told him nothing. So he said:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("No'm-well, not very much.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The old lady reached out her hand and felt Tom's shirt, and said:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("But you ain't too warm now, though." And it flattered her to reflect) Tj
T*
(that she had discovered that the shirt was dry without anybody knowing) Tj
T*
(that that was what she had in her mind. But in spite of her, Tom knew) Tj
T*
(where the wind lay, now. So he forestalled what might be the next move:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("Some of us pumped on our heads-mine's damp yet. See?") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Aunt Polly was vexed to think she had overlooked that bit of) Tj
T*
(circumstantial evidence, and missed a trick. Then she had a new) Tj
T*
(inspiration:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("Tom, you didn't have to undo your shirt collar where I sewed it, to) Tj
T*
(pump on your head, did you? Unbutton your jacket!") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(The trouble vanished out of Tom's face. He opened his jacket. His shirt) Tj
T*
(collar was securely sewed.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
("Bother! Well, go 'long with you. I'd made sure you'd played hookey) Tj
T*
(and been a-swimming. But I forgive ye, Tom. I reckon you're a kind of a) Tj
T*
(singed cat, as the saying is-better'n you look. _This_ time.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(She was half sorry her sagacity had miscarried, and half glad that Tom) Tj
T*
(had stumbled into obedient conduct for once.) Tj
